10690 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (a. Binary Locks: A simple mechanism where a data item can be either locked \(in use\) or unlocked.If a thread tries to acquire the lock when it's already locked, it must wait until the lock is released by the thread currently holding it.)
/Pg 479 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
10691 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (b. Shared and Exclusive Locks)
/Pg 479 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
10692 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17212 0 R
/ActualText (Shared Lock \(S-lock\): Allows multiple transactions to read a data item simultaneously but prevents any of them from modifying it. Multiple transactions can hold a shared lock on the same data item at the same time.)
/Pg 479 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
10693 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 17213 0 R
/ActualText (Exclusive Lock \(X-lock\): Allows a transaction to both read and modify a data item. When an exclusive lock is held by a transaction, no other transaction can read or modify the data item.)
/Pg 479 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
10694 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 3623 0 R
/Pg 480 0 R
>>
endobj
10695 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T <4C4554905320535441525420574954482044424D53203A29>
/E <4C4554905320535441525420574954482044424D53203A29>
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
10696 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Concurrency control mechanisms)
/E (Concurrency control mechanisms)
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
10697 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Note : When a transaction acquires a shared lock on a data item, other transactions can also acquire shared locks on that same item, enabling concurrent reads. However, no transaction can acquire an exclusive lock on that item as long as one or more shared locks are held.)
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
10698 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (When a transaction acquires an exclusive lock on a data item, it has full control over that item, meaning it can both read and modify it. No other transaction can acquire a lock on the same data item until the exclusive lock is released.)
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
10699 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Shared lock -> any no of transactions)
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
10700 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Exclusive lock -> only one transaction should hold it at one time)
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
10701 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (While shared and exclusive locks are vital for maintaining data integrity and consistency in concurrent environments, they can introduce significant challenges in terms of performance, deadlocks, reduced concurrency, and system complexity. )
/Pg 480 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
